---
<BaseLayout
  extraJsonLd={[techArticleJsonLd]}
  title="Power Generation System: Diesel-Electric Rigs, SCR"
  description="How a rig's power generation system works: diesel generators, the diesel-electric SCR/VFD model most modern rigs use, and why it replaced mechanical drive."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Equipment', href: '/equipment' }, { label: 'Power Generation System', href: '/equipment/power-generation-system' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/equipment/power-generation-system"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/equipment/power-generation-system" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Equipment — Core Systems</div>
    <h1>Power Generation <span>System</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">The diesel generators and electrical distribution that power everything else on the rig — drawworks, mud pumps, and top drive alike.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-it-is">What the Power Generation System Does</h2>
        <p>Every other system on the rig — <a href="/equipment/hoisting-system">hoisting</a>, <a href="/equipment/rotating-system">rotating</a>, and <a href="/equipment/fluid-circulation-system">fluid circulation</a> — needs real power to actually move, and on most modern rigs that power comes from a shared electrical supply rather than each system having its own independent mechanical drive. Diesel engines drive generators, the generators feed a shared electrical bus, and individual pieces of equipment draw from that bus as needed.</p>

        <KeyFacts
          lang="en"
          rows={[["Prime movers", "Diesel engine–generator sets (some dual-fuel or natural gas); three to five on a typical land rig, more on offshore units."], ["Supply", "AC, commonly 600 V on land rigs; offshore units often generate at medium voltage. 60 Hz in the Americas, 50 Hz in most other regions."], ["Drive systems", "SCR (AC rectified to DC for DC motors) or VFD (variable-frequency AC for AC motors)."], ["Main loads", "Drawworks, mud pumps, top drive or rotary table; plus solids control, lighting and camp power."], ["Backup", "Spare generating capacity for one set out of service, plus an emergency generator; offshore units add UPS power for critical systems."]]}
          standards={["api500"]}
          standardsNote={"There is no single API specification for rig generators; API RP 500 governs where electrical equipment may be placed around the wellhead, shakers and pits."}
        />

        <h2 id="diesel-electric">Why Diesel-Electric Rather Than Direct Mechanical Drive</h2>
        <p>Older rigs, and some still in service, ran direct mechanical drive: diesel engines connected through chains, belts, or a compound drive directly to the drawworks and pumps, with clutches engaging whichever equipment needed power at a given moment. That works, but it's inflexible — the mechanical linkage between engine and equipment is fixed, and balancing power across multiple pieces of equipment running simultaneously is a real constraint.</p>
        <p>A <strong>diesel-electric</strong> rig sidesteps that by converting mechanical power to electrical power once, at the generators, then distributing it electrically to wherever it's needed. The drawworks, mud pumps, and top drive can each draw exactly the power they need from the same shared pool, and adding or removing load from one system doesn't require re-engaging a physical clutch on another. This flexibility is the main reason most rigs built in recent decades are diesel-electric.</p>

        <h2 id="scr-vfd">SCR and VFD Systems</h2>
        <p><strong>SCR (Silicon Controlled Rectifier)</strong> systems were the first generation of this approach: the generators produce AC power, which the SCR system converts to DC to drive DC motors on the drawworks and mud pumps. "SCR rig" became industry shorthand for this entire class of diesel-electric rig, even on equipment that has since moved past the SCR component itself.</p>
        <p><strong>VFD (Variable Frequency Drive)</strong> systems are the more modern approach: instead of converting to DC, a VFD converts the generators' fixed-frequency AC into a variable-frequency AC supply that drives modern AC motors at precisely controlled speed. AC motors under VFD control need less maintenance than the DC motors an SCR system drives, and give finer, more responsive speed control — which is why VFD has become the standard on newly built rigs.</p>

        <h2 id="redundancy">Generator Redundancy and Failure Consequences</h2>
        <p>A total power loss stops the drawworks, mud pumps, and top drive simultaneously — there's no independent backup path for any of them once the shared electrical supply is down. That's exactly why rigs run more generator capacity than the minimum needed at any one time: enough redundancy that one unit can be pulled for maintenance without losing total available power, and enough reserve that starting up an additional big load (like the mud pumps ramping up) doesn't itself risk an overload. A power failure with pipe partially out of the hole is a genuinely serious situation, since the hoisting system's auxiliary brake and continued mud circulation both depend on that same power supply.</p>

      </div>

